Z||000000||wayne||06-13-99||07:37 PM||||Any suggetion on how to improve brakes on a 85 GT.<p>I saw a message about a recall on the <br>E brake, I'm going to check on this tommorw.<p>If the E brake is not worknig can this effect normal braking? (of course mine doesn't work.)<p>I heard somehting about using grand am brakes is this OK?<p>wayne
Z||000001||batboy||06-13-99||08:47 PM||||I'm no expert on brakes, so I hope someone who has more experience will jump in and add to my suggestions or correct me if neccesary. Probably the easiest and cheapest way to improve Fiero brakes is to install those high performance "sticky" brake pads, carbomet I think is what they are called. Of course, make sure the calipers are working properly and inspect the brake lines/hoses, plus have a shop turn the rotors if needed. The next option, if you have the money, is to install those special cross drilled rotors with the carbomet pads. The Fiero Store and other places sell these, shop around. I believe Ocelot is one of the brand names. Swapping different brakes (such as the Grand Am's) requires more expertise, knowledge, and usually a machine shop to modify stuff. Look at some of the other past forum discussions for an excellent link to a web site that describes this procedure. Good luck.
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||06-13-99||09:54 PM||||I think the Grand Am brakes are a complete bolt on. The parts cost half as much as Fiero parts. This conversion eliminates your parking brake. The front Grand Am brakes are what is used so you have NO PARKING BRAKE what so ever. If you have a manual tranny it may come back to haunt you during inspection.
Z||000003||batboy||06-13-99||10:48 PM||||SCCA Fiero, according to the article I read about the Grand Am brake swap (I personally don't know) the back brakes are a bolt on, you do lose your e-brake as you mentioned, but the front brakes require modification in the form of machining the old rotors down to use them as hubs so the Grand Am rotors will fit over them (like the rear rotors that are standard on Fieros). This also moves the wheel out a little plus you may have to use longer lug bolts. Check out this link www.qtm.net/~fierogt/suspensionmodz.htm for more info. 
Z||000004||theogre||06-14-99||01:41 PM||||Unless you are very familiar with doing brake work, use one of the available kits. There are a number of them, some of them are only a little more than stock parts for a complete rebuild. Even a basic kit that has better pads and cross drilled rotors will provide a huge improvement.<p>The brake recall applied to most 84/85/86 cars as I remember. it replaces the rear calipers with one that have better e brake screws. The problem is not generally known to have caused main braking problems.<p>also, if you have Alloy wheels make sure the contact area between the wheel and rotor is clean. That's a nice big aluminum heat sink bolted up and it pulls an amazing amount of heat if the surfaces are kept clean. DO NOT oil or grease the surfaces to prevent corosion! Lubricant here can cause major problems.
Z||000005||Phil||06-15-99||07:42 AM||||Maybe someone can clear something up for me. It was my understanding that cross drilled rotors were best left for racing, their purpose is to dissapate heat more quickly (good thing) but they have a tendency to develope cracks. Race cars change parts very frenquently therefore the cracking problem is of no concern. Any thoughts? 
Z||000006||PaUL||06-15-99||10:28 AM||||I think a big improvment in the brakes would be vented rotors instead of the stock solid ones like the 88s have. I was once looking at an old X car rotor and noticed it was the same diameter as the fieros, but the X car rotor was vented and much thicker, so you wouldnt be able to use the fiero calipers. Ive read about the Grand Am brakes but it sounds like a big hassle.  
Z||000007||theogre||06-15-99||05:47 PM||||[u]Properly[/u] drilled rotors will actually last longer as the holes help with stress management. Holes that are too many, too big, too large, or badly placed, will cause failure sooner as they weeken the rotor. You can't just throw a rotor in an indexed mill and start making holes or grooves.<p>Cross drilling and venting serve diferant perposes. <p>The perpose of venting is to remove heat from the rotor. Venting does this, rather obviously, by pumping air thru the rotor.<p>The primarly perpose of drilling is not heat disipation, but rather waste removal. In operation brake pads generate waste gasses and dust that can lift the pads off the rotor. The holes remove this waste and prevent lifting of the pads. The holes improvement to heat disipation in a solid rotor is negligable.<p>If you look at some of the diferant brakes used by NASCAR you will find all sorts of rotors, including solid ones. There are even vented rotors that are also drilled. It all depends on what track they are on what ones they use.<p>On semi trucks raced in Europe, they even use water injection to provide further cooling. These trucks can consume over 200 gallons of water in one race.<p>There is a "lost" technique that was often used in drum brakes. in these the shoe was milled with a groove down the center(long way) of the friction material. This grove did the same thing as the holes in disk rotors. I've seen this done to disk pads, but like cross drilling, it has to be carefully planned or you have problems. (Like the pad disintegrating.)
Z||000008||GREENDANO||06-15-99||05:49 PM||||From what I understand, cross-drilled rotors on a street driven car are cool looking but nothing else.  Even on you car's worst day you wouldn't need the benefits of cross drilled rotors.  <br>  I installed the above mentioned Grand Am brakes on my GT last year.  All I can say is WOW!!!!  I almost chipped a tooth on the steering wheel the first time I stopped hard with the new brakes.  Major improvement!!  The rear was pretty much a bolt-on, only a little grinding, plus I had to bend the rotor splash shield away from the rotor.  The front brakes were a different story.  I agree with TheOgre,  if you're not familiar with brakes, don't mess around.  If you are familiar, then by all means do the swap.  I have an auto, so my e-brake had never been used by the previous owner and was seized when I got the car two years ago.  The hardest part was cutting the original rotor down to make it into a hub.  Actually it wasn't hard, just time consuming.  The bottom line is, my little GT now has "super brakes" to go with all my other performance mods.<br>  Dan-O
Z||000009||batboy||06-15-99||06:30 PM||||Dan-o says crossed-drilled rotors are a waste of money on a street Fireo, but is a little vague on why. Ogre says they're a big improvement and states a couple of reasons why they are better. So, before I spend my hard earned bucks on them, who is right? I think the Grand Am brake swap is too much hassle (at least for me). Is there an alternative? PaUL, what about those X car rotors? If used with the right caliper, I wonder if those would be an easier swap? I once swapped early 70's Monte Carlo disk brakes onto a 68 Camaro by switching the entire spindle, steering knuckle, rotor, and caliper as a unit. It turned out to be a bolt on. What are the chances of that happening with a Fiero? If so, what would be a possible donor car? It would probably have to be an "A frame" front-end type of vehicle, not a front drive car (or would it?). Any thoughts guys?
Z||000010||PaUL||06-15-99||07:24 PM||||The X car vented rotors and calipers will bolt up to the rear of the Fiero but not the front. I read somewhere that something about the hub carriers in the front wont allow it to fit. So whats the use? who wants to have vented rotors in the back but not the front.
