Z||000012||theogre||06-16-99||12:59 AM||||Actually,,,,,, having improved breaking on the back only, especialy with a HP upgrade, is quite a sound thing to do.<p>Here's an example. I used to own a 1973 Ford Maverick. These cars usually came with an inline 6 cyl motor. Mine had been "upgraded" to a 289cid v8 and an "adjusted" C-4 transmission. Basically this car would burn the tires to the rim and not move 10 feet and there in is also it's problem........<br>trying to keep the rear wheels from turning when this car was stoped on anything besides totaly dry pavement required BOTH feet on the break pedal.<p>To eliminate this problem I had to install rear break cylinders off of a Grand Torino which where 2/32" bigger bore than the original Maverick cylinders. (OEM was 27/32" the Torino used 29/32")<p>The most difficult thing to do to a car is to make it stop in a balanced, SAFE, manor. Any jackass can make a car go fast but to make a fast car steer and stop is an art form.<p>
Z||000013||lowCG||06-16-99||03:51 PM||||I fully agree with that,Ogre,I am pretty much set on making a world class Fiero,because I just know it can be done ,and won't be happy until I've achieved in all areas.<br>I imagine that 4-piston calipers would have a better "feel" than single,floating calipers.<br>I'm currently checking out the Toyota brakes for performance use.The 4WD truck models have been using 4-piston calipers for awhile now,and the T100/Tundra ones could be large enough for serious duty with 12" rotors.Will let everyone know if I find any cool deals.
Z||000014||Racer 1||06-16-99||04:36 PM||||Has anyone tried those carbon metalic brake pads offered by fiero store?Also fiero store sells drilled rotors.And PISA corp also sell the stock 88' fiero rotor setup for the earleir model which also includes a safety parking brake so theives cant steel your car.I also remeber seeing someone else making 13" front and 12" rear brake conversion kit.But you have to have 15 or bigger rims.I have to look in focoa back issues.... 
Z||000015||batboy||06-16-99||05:02 PM||||Here is an updated article about swapping the Baretta/Grand Am brakes to a Fiero:  members.tripod.com/Randy_Agree/brakes2.html<p>I may change my mind about this swap, IF (that's a big IF) I can find a reputable machine shop to modify the old Fiero rotors into hubs by turning them down on a lathe. Sounds better than spending over $1000 for those PISA brakes. Heck, that's nearly half of what I paid for the whole car. I like the idea about using those 4 piston calipers, but I bet it'll be a nightmare trying to engineer those onto a Fiero. Remember, if you use any rotor other than a GM (in order to match the caliper), the wheel bolt pattern will probably be different, no thanks.
